---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="Screen reader automation that starts in the CLI"
   summary="a11ied is built for the awkward middle ground most accessibility tooling skips: not just static rules, not just raw screen reader driving, but a workflow that can explain the criterion, check whether it applies, gather evidence, and stay honest about what is still manual."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section class="split panel">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Start from the job</p>
            <h2>How people actually arrive here</h2>
         </div>
         <div class="workflow-list">
            <div class="workflow-row">
               <strong><a href="/workflows#check-one-criterion">Check one criterion</a></strong>
               <span>Look up the rule, scope the target, gather evidence, then make the claim.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-row">
               <strong><a href="/workflows#verify-a-level">Verify a target level</a></strong>
               <span>Use the level workflow when you need the whole AA or AAA matrix instead of one rule.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-row">
               <strong><a href="/workflows#drive-a-session">Drive a reader session</a></strong>
               <span>Use the low-level driver surface when you need manual control over VoiceOver, NVDA, or the virtual reader.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-row">
               <strong><a href="/workflows#agent-and-storybook">Use it from an agent or Storybook</a></strong>
               <span>Start with MCP or Storybook when the target is already wrapped in an editor or component workflow.</span>
            </div>
         </div>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">field rule</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            The docs are easier to trust when they tell you where to start for a real job, not when they
            ask you to reverse-engineer the repo architecture first.
         </p>
         <p class="aside-note">
            If you already know the command tree, jump straight to <a href="/cli-reference">CLI
            reference</a> or <a href="/mcp-usage">MCP usage</a>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Operator ladder</p>
         <h2>Use the product without getting lost</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="mini-grid">
         <div class="stack">
            <div class="tag">1. Look up the rule</div>
            <p>Use <code>wcag</code> first. Pull the criterion, coverage, and strategy before you touch the target.</p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <div class="tag">2. Scope the target</div>
            <p>Use <code>inspect</code> to decide whether the rule belongs on this page or story at all.</p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <div class="tag">3. Gather evidence</div>
            <p>Use <code>drive</code>, <code>run axe</code>, or <code>run pattern</code> depending on how raw the procedure needs to be.</p>
         </div>
         <div class="stack">
            <div class="tag">4. Make the claim</div>
            <p>Use <code>verify</code> last, once you know exactly what the runtime did and did not cover.</p>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Five-minute path</p>
         <h2>Fast start</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="command-strip">
         <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js doctor
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag show 4.1.3 --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js inspect criterion 4.1.3 --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js run pattern status_message_probe --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js verify criterion 4.1.3 --target virtual --url http://127.0.0.1:6173/status-message.html --json</code></pre>
         <p class="command-note">
         That ladder is the shape of the product. If you jump from "I think this is a status message
         issue" straight to <code>verify</code>, the output gets a lot harder to trust.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Reference map</p>
         <h2>What lives where</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/workflows">Workflows</a></strong>
            <span>The task-first page. Start here if you know the job but not the route names yet.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/cli-reference">CLI reference</a></strong>
            <span>The full command tree, including <code>help-all</code> for agents that want the whole surface in one dump.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/wcag-data-sources">WCAG data sources</a></strong>
            <span>Where the standards data comes from and why the repo keeps it local.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/criterion-lookup">Criterion lookup</a> and <a href="/applicability">Applicability</a></strong>
            <span>The planning layer. Figure out what rule you mean and whether it matters here.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/driver-usage">Driver usage</a> and <a href="/pattern-execution">Pattern execution</a></strong>
            <span>The evidence layer, from raw screen reader control to reusable probes.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/verification-semantics">Verification semantics</a></strong>
            <span>The claim layer. This is where the runtime says pass, fail, mixed, manual-only, or not-covered.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/mcp-usage">MCP usage</a> and <a href="/storybook-usage">Storybook usage</a></strong>
            <span>The agent path and the component path.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/api-reference">API reference</a> and <a href="/recording-sessions">Recording sessions</a></strong>
            <span>The public package map and the real-target video trail.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Honesty rule</p>
         <h2>The rule underneath all of this</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         a11ied is allowed to collect strong evidence. It is not allowed to bluff. Plenty of tools get
         mushy here. This repo tries very hard not to.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
